[Event "Opening Traps for Black"]
[Lesson "trapped-bishop"]
[LessonTitle "The trapped bishop (Noah's Ark Trap)"]
[Step "explain"]
[TeacherNotes "Name matches the Lichess list: Ruy Lopez, Noah's Ark Trap | The idea: White's bishop on b3 has no safe squares because its own pawns and Black's pawns block it | The mistake is 8.Qxd4 (taking back the pawn with the queen) | After ...c5 the queen must move, and ...c4 traps the bishop | Black wins a bishop for two pawns | Black needs to know the pawn moves ...b5 and ...d6 are solid on their own"]
[Mistakes "Playing ...c5 before the knights are traded on d4 | Forgetting the move ...Be6 which also develops a piece | Missing that c4 attacks the bishop and every exit is closed"]
[ClassQuestions "Which squares could the bishop on b3 go to? Why does each fail? | Why is 8.Qxd4 a mistake? | What does the pawn on c4 attack?"]

{In this lesson White's bishop will end up on b3 with very few places to go. Black first trades off the center with the knight, then uses pawns to trap the bishop. The trap starts when White takes back on d4 with the queen.}
1. e4 e5 2. Nf3 Nc6 3. Bb5 a6 4. Ba4 b5 5. Bb3 {The bishop steps back to b3. Remember this square. [%csl Rb3]} 5... d6 6. d4 Nxd4 7. Nxd4 exd4 8. Qxd4 {White takes back the pawn with the queen. This is the mistake. [%csl Rd4]} 8... c5 {The pawn attacks the queen. [%cal Gc5d4]} 9. Qd5 {The queen runs to d5.} 9... Be6 {The bishop attacks the queen again. [%cal Ge6d5]} 10. Qc6+ {The queen checks.} 10... Bd7 {The bishop blocks and attacks the queen again. [%cal Gd7c6]} 11. Qd5 {The queen goes back.} 11... c4 {Now the pawn attacks the bishop on b3, and it has no safe square. White loses a piece. [%cal Gc5c4,Rc4b3] [%csl Rb3]} *

[Event "Opening Traps for Black"]
[Lesson "trapped-bishop"]
[Step "find-move"]
[FEN "r1bqkbnr/2p2ppp/p2p4/1p6/3QP3/1B6/PPP2PPP/RNB1K2R b KQkq - 0 8"]
[Hint "Use a pawn."]
[Hint "The pawn on c7 can attack the queen."]
[Hint "Play c7 to c5."]

{White took the pawn on d4 with the queen. Black to move. Find the pawn move that chases the queen and starts the trap.}
8... c5 {The queen is attacked. The bishop on b3 will soon be in trouble. [%cal Gc7c5,Gc5d4]} 9. Qd5 Be6 {The bishop attacks the queen.} 10. Qc6+ Bd7 {The bishop blocks the check and attacks the queen.} 11. Qd5 c4 {The pawn attacks the bishop on b3, and it cannot escape.} *

[Event "Opening Traps for Black"]
[Lesson "trapped-bishop"]
[Step "find-move"]
[FEN "r2qkbnr/3b1ppp/p2p4/1ppQ4/4P3/1B6/PPP2PPP/RNB1K2R b KQkq - 5 11"]
[Hint "Use a pawn."]
[Hint "Look at the bishop on b3 and the pawn on c5."]
[Hint "Play c5 to c4."]

{The queen is on d5 again. Find the move that traps the white bishop.}
11... c4 {The pawn on c4 attacks the bishop. The bishop cannot go to a4 because the b5 pawn guards it. The squares a2 and c2 hold White pawns, and if the bishop takes c4, the b pawn takes it. [%cal Gc5c4,Rc4b3] [%csl Ra4,Ra2,Rc2]} *

[Event "Opening Traps for Black"]
[Lesson "trapped-bishop"]
[Step "line-drill"]
[Side "black"]
[Hint "Black plays a6, b5 and d6, three solid pawn moves."]
[Hint "Trade the knights on d4, then play a pawn to c5 to chase the queen."]
[Hint "Finish with Be6, Bd7 and then the pawn to c4."]

{Play the whole trap as Black. It only works if White takes on d4 with the queen.}
1. e4 e5 2. Nf3 Nc6 3. Bb5 a6 4. Ba4 b5 5. Bb3 d6 6. d4 Nxd4 7. Nxd4 exd4 8. Qxd4 c5 9. Qd5 Be6 10. Qc6+ Bd7 11. Qd5 c4 *

[Event "Opening Traps for Black"]
[Lesson "trapped-bishop"]
[Step "quiz"]
[Question "White to move. Which move falls into the trap?"]
[Choices "Qxd4 (take the pawn with the queen) | c3 (offer a pawn to open lines)"]
[Answer "1"]
[Explanation "8.Qxd4 walks into ...c5 and the trapped bishop. A different move such as 8.c3 (a pawn offer) does not fall into this trap."]

{Think about what the queen would face after taking the pawn.}
1. e4 e5 2. Nf3 Nc6 3. Bb5 a6 4. Ba4 b5 5. Bb3 d6 6. d4 Nxd4 7. Nxd4 exd4 *

[Event "Opening Traps for Black"]
[Lesson "trapped-bishop"]
[Step "quiz"]
[Question "Why does the white bishop on b3 have no escape after ...c4?"]
[Choices "Its own pawns and Black's pawns block its squares | Bishops cannot move backwards | Black's queen guards every square"]
[Answer "1"]
[Explanation "The pawn on b5 covers a4, and White's own pawns block a2 and c2. If the bishop takes c4, the b5 pawn takes it."]

{Look at the squares around the bishop.}
1. e4 e5 2. Nf3 Nc6 3. Bb5 a6 4. Ba4 b5 5. Bb3 d6 6. d4 Nxd4 7. Nxd4 exd4 8. Qxd4 c5 9. Qd5 Be6 10. Qc6+ Bd7 11. Qd5 c4 *
